Data structures and algorithms form the backbone of computer science and software engineering.
Understanding how data is organized and manipulated is essential for writing efficient programs.

A data structure is a particular way of organizing data in a computer so that it can be used
effectively. Different kinds of data structures are suited to different kinds of applications,
and some are highly specialized to specific tasks. For example, relational databases commonly
use B-tree indexes for data retrieval, while compiler implementations usually use hash tables
to look up identifiers.

An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure for solving a problem or accomplishing a task. The
efficiency of an algorithm is measured in terms of time complexity and space complexity. Time
complexity describes the amount of time an algorithm takes to complete as a function of the
input size, while space complexity describes the amount of memory space required.

Huffman coding is a lossless data compression algorithm. The idea is to assign variable-length
codes to input characters, with lengths based on frequencies of corresponding characters. The
most frequent character gets the smallest code, and the least frequent character gets the largest
code. This is similar to Morse code, where the most common letter in English, 'e', has the
shortest code.

The algorithm works by creating a binary tree of nodes. Initially, all nodes are leaf nodes,
which contain the character itself, the weight (frequency) of the character. Internal nodes
contain a weight and links to two child nodes. As a convention, bit '0' represents following
the left child and bit '1' represents following the right child. A finished tree has up to n
leaf nodes and n-1 internal nodes.

Trees are fundamental data structures in computer science. A tree is a hierarchical data
structure that consists of nodes connected by edges. The topmost node is called the root, and
the nodes at the bottom are called leaves. Each node can have zero or more child nodes.

Binary trees are trees in which each node has at most two children, referred to as the left
child and the right child. Binary search trees are a special kind of binary tree where the
left child contains values less than the parent, and the right child contains values greater
than the parent.

Priority queues are abstract data types that are similar to regular queues but each element has
a priority associated with it. An element with high priority is served before an element with
low priority. Priority queues are commonly implemented using heaps, which are complete binary
trees that satisfy the heap property.

In a min-heap, the key of each node is less than or equal to the keys of its children. This
means the minimum element is always at the root. In a max-heap, the key of each node is greater
than or equal to the keys of its children, so the maximum element is at the root.

Graphs are non-linear data structures consisting of vertices (nodes) and edges that connect
pairs of vertices. Graphs can be directed or undirected, weighted or unweighted. Common graph
traversal algorithms include Breadth-First Search (BFS) and Depth-First Search (DFS).

Hash tables provide O(1) average-case time complexity for insertion, deletion, and lookup
operations. They work by using a hash function to compute an index into an array of buckets,
from which the desired value can be found. Collision resolution techniques include chaining
and open addressing.

Sorting algorithms are fundamental to computer science. Common sorting algorithms include
bubble sort, selection sort, insertion sort, merge sort, quick sort, and heap sort. The choice
of sorting algorithm depends on factors such as the size of the dataset, whether the data is
partially sorted, and memory constraints.

Dynamic programming is a method for solving complex problems by breaking them down into simpler
subproblems. It is applicable when the subproblems overlap, meaning the same subproblems are
solved multiple times. By storing the results of subproblems, dynamic programming avoids
redundant computation and significantly improves efficiency.

